WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:03.360
Did Madonna really make Weird Al Yankovic
parody her music?

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.120
And did he really compose his first songs
in the bathroom?

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.340
Here's what the bizarre sort-of-biopic Weird:
The Al Yankovic Story actually manages to

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:11.340
get right.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:14.880
"Hope you guys are ready for this.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.480
YAHHH!"

00:00:17.623 --> 00:00:22.060
Al’s parents Nick and Mary Yankovic are
depicted as harsh and authoritarian in Weird:

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:23.340
The Al Yankovic Story.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:28.609
The portrayal is greatly exaggerated for comic
effect, but there is some truth to their strictness.

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:32.329
According to the San Luis Obispo Tribune,
Al’s religious upbringing caused him to

00:00:32.329 --> 00:00:35.170
abstain from drugs, alcohol and even swearing.

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:37.329
Mary controlled her son in other ways, too.

00:00:37.329 --> 00:00:41.730
The young Al — short for Alfred — was
not allowed to visit friends’ homes or have sleepovers.

00:00:41.788 --> 00:00:45.660
And he was only permitted to ride his bike
to his aunt’s house just half a block away.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.930
Even then, Mary would watch from the front
lawn, embodying the term "helicopter parent"

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:52.340
some 20 years before it entered common parlance.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:56.140
However, despite her best efforts, Mary could
not come between Al and his favorite disk

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:59.739
jockey Dr. Demento, whom she considered "inappropriate."

00:00:59.739 --> 00:01:04.093
To avoid her earshot, Al lowered his radio’s
volume and took it under the bedsheets.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:08.610
The film depicts a door-to-door salesman visiting
the Yankovic home and introducing the young

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:10.670
Al to guitars and accordions.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:14.610
This is true, according to the New York Times
Magazine, although it was not Al’s choice

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:15.610
of instrument.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:19.200
In real life, that decision was made by his
mother Mary, who chose the accordion because

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.330
Frankie Yankovic, no relation, was America’s
reigning "Polka King."

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:25.920
Guitars may have been cooler, but Al really
took to his accordion.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:31.610
The first lesson was on October 22, 1966,
the day before Al’s seventh birthday, and

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:36.000
his teacher showed him the difference between
the beginner, intermediate, and adult sized accordions.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.240
With so much time spent at home, Al made quick
progress with his accordion and, by early

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.409
adolescence, he was able to play along to
some of his favorite albums.

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:45.620
Al’s accordion also helped him overcome
his shyness.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.409
At college, Al would perform in the dorm room
at his roommates’ behest, breaking awkward

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:51.140
barriers in the process.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:52.890
"Anyone got an accordion?"

00:01:54.507 --> 00:01:57.139
Al’s conception of "My Bologna" is one of
the film’s funniest sequences.

00:01:57.139 --> 00:02:02.409
The curly maestro is making sandwiches for
his housemates when inspiration strikes.

00:02:02.409 --> 00:02:06.571
As The Knack’s "My Sharona" plays on the
radio, Al’s eyes meet with a pack of bologna

00:02:06.571 --> 00:02:11.090
and, as if through divine intervention, the
DJ’s record appears to stick and repeats

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:12.819
the chorus several times.

00:02:12.819 --> 00:02:16.730
Yankovic’s inspiration gets stronger with
every repetition and, before his friends have

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:20.830
time to change the station, Al rushes to his
accordion to give the first performance of

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:21.849
"My Bologna."

00:02:21.849 --> 00:02:25.690
Enthralled, Al and his bandmates rush to make
a recording and they choose a bus station

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:28.060
toilet because of the favorable acoustics.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.620
This is true, although it wasn’t at a bus
station.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:35.030
Al actually made the recording in a men’s
bathroom at California Polytechnic State University.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:38.850
Yankovic likely got some strange looks but
the unorthodoxy was worth it.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:43.150
"My Bologna" did so well on the Dr. Demento
show that it reached The Knack, whose song

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.700
"My Sharona" had inspired Al’s goofy cover.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:49.040
Some time later, Yankovic met with the band
during a show at Cal Poly.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.760
Lead vocalist Doug Feiger received Al warmly,
praising his cover as a "really great song"

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.240
and advising Rupert Perry, the then vice president
of Capitol Records, to get Yankovic on board.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:02.059
It was at this moment that Weird Al believed
he had a shot in the business.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:07.104
The film sees "Weird Al'" perform his first
show in a rough punk joint full of heckling thugs.

00:03:07.104 --> 00:03:11.090
After an unpromising start, Al hits his stride
and the crowd goes wild.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:14.190
This scene is a mix-up as far as accuracy
is concerned.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:18.650
Al did stir crowds early in his career, but
the first instance occurred on campus at Cal

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:20.390
Poly, not a boozy dive bar.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:23.460
It was an open-mic night some time in 1977.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:27.260
After his countercultural peers did their
thing, Al took to the stage with his friend

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:30.989
Joel Miller and played an eclectic 10-minute
medley that was supposed to be a cover of

00:03:30.989 --> 00:03:32.310
every song in history.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:33.500
The crowd loved it.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:35.950
There were cheers, applause and a standing
ovation.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:40.310
It was the first time that Yankovic had ever
had such “positive reinforcement," according

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:42.720
to an interview in the New York Times Magazine.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.989
Consequently, with the help of an LA manager,
Al landed a show that bore a greater resemblance

00:03:46.989 --> 00:03:48.390
to the dive we see in the film.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:53.666
The venue was a large nightclub in Phoenix
and, like at Cal Poly, the audience loved him.

00:03:53.666 --> 00:03:55.584
However, it wasn’t all plain sailing.

00:03:55.584 --> 00:04:00.110
When Al and his band opened for Missing Persons
at the Santa Monica Civic Auditorium, they

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:02.050
endured 45 minutes of abuse.

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:05.846
Instead of applause, there were cries of “Get
off the stage!” and “YOU SUCK!”

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:11.239
With his top hat and eccentric demeanor, Dr.
Demento may strike the unfamiliar viewer as

00:04:11.239 --> 00:04:13.640
another figment of Yankovic’s lively imagination.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:17.359
But Demento is a real individual and he really
did "discover" Yankovic.

00:04:17.359 --> 00:04:21.299
He didn’t meet Yankovic at a live event,
though, but on the airwaves.

00:04:21.299 --> 00:04:26.180
In 1976, Yankovic sent Demento a recording
of "Belvedere Cruising," a song he wrote about

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:27.860
his family’s rickety old car.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:30.240
Demento accepted it, playing the jingle on
air.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:34.870
Yankovic followed this up with numerous other
mixtapes, namely the aforementioned "My Bologna,"

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:38.539
which was a hit on Demento’s show and then
with listeners nationwide.

00:04:38.539 --> 00:04:42.176
"That became viral in the days before things
went viral."

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:46.930
In Weird: The Al Yankovic Story, Madonna is
depicted as a musician of secondary status

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:49.770
who latches on to Yankovic to further her
own career.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:53.020
The inverse is true, but it’s all part of
the film’s rise and fall farce.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:56.060
There is no proof that Madonna and Weird Al
have even met.

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:59.380
"It's obvious that there's something going
on with me and Madonna, so everyone's like,

00:04:59.537 --> 00:05:01.017
'Did that really happen?'"

00:05:01.017 --> 00:05:05.090
However, there is a sliver of truth in how
the film shows Madonna inquiring about a Weird

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:07.300
Al cover of her song, "Like a Virgin."

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.370
In an interview with Soundcheck, Yankovic
said,

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:13.060
"Madonna was actually the person who suggested
that I do the parody 'Like a Surgeon.'"

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:17.120
Apparently, Madonna was walking with a friend
when she mentioned Weird Al, suggesting that

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:18.900
his cover could be "Like a Surgeon."

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.139
Fortunately, this friend knew Al’s manager.

00:05:21.139 --> 00:05:25.520
Yankovic has made a point of conceiving his
own cover lyrics, but Madonna's rhyme was

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:26.580
just too good.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:31.460
The parody single was released in the summer
of 1985, reached number 47 on the Billboard

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:34.560
100, and became one of Yankovic’s most enduring
songs.

